Accept switches with non-zero padding.

Class loading rejected a tableswitch or lookupswitch whose alignment
padding held anything but zero bytes. Some older compilers left other
values there, so whole programs failed to start with a ClassFormatError.
The interpreter, the compiler and the verifier all skip the padding and
read the aligned operands, which keep their bounds checks.

--- a/cldc/src/vm/share/handles/Method.cpp
+++ b/cldc/src/vm/share/handles/Method.cpp
@@ -1074,12 +1074,8 @@
             if (a_bci + 2 * wordSize > codesize) {
               goto error;
             }
-            //CR6538939: only zero-byte padding is allowed
-            for (int offset = bci; ++offset < a_bci; ) {
-              if ((jubyte)bytecode_at_raw(offset) != 0) {
-                goto error;
-              }
-            }
+            // The padding is never read, so its bytes need not be zero:
+            // older compilers left other values there.
             int fields;
             if (code == Bytecodes::_tableswitch) {
               // Bounds check: should be able to read at least 3 words
